from _lib import *

WEB2 = module("WEB2", "Web 2: making a web app safe - the defender's checklist on your own app", "Security",
  "You built a web app. Now you make it safe. Every common web weakness is one place where an app trusted something it should have checked. You will go through your own noticeboard, confirm each defence is in place, and explain why it works - which is exactly what a web security engineer checks on a real app.",
  "Abid", "A short 'security review' of her own noticeboard, written like a professional checklist: what she checked, why each defence works, what she would check next - sent to Abid as a PDF from her logbook.",
  [
    lesson("WEB2-L1", "The one idea: input is data, never instructions",
      ["State the rule: everything arriving from outside (form fields, the URL, cookies, headers) is data to be checked, never trusted as commands", "List the entry points in her own app and what a sensible check is for each", "Explain why checks live on the server, since anyone can skip the browser's"],
      FROM_ZERO + LINUX + LAB + "This is the whole module in one sentence, and she already met it as the Boundary Question. Walk her own noticeboard code with her: where does outside data come in (the form's who/text, the URL, the login cookie)? For each, what is the right check (not empty, length cap, the right type, the right owner)? Why server-side: the browser's checks are a convenience for honest users; the server's are the real wall, because a request can come from curl with no browser at all - she already proved this in WEB1-L3. No attacking anything: this lesson is the map of her own app's edges. Check: the rule in her words, and three entry points with their checks."),
    lesson("WEB2-L2", "Text that could become a tag: escaping, and why her noticeboard is already safe",
      ["Explain the weakness in one line (if a note's text is put on the page without escaping, a browser could read it as page code) and point to the exact line in her app that prevents it", "Confirm html.escape (WEB1) is applied to every place user text reaches the page, and add a template that escapes by default", "Explain the defence in depth: escaping on output, plus a Content-Security-Policy header"],
      FROM_ZERO + LINUX + LAB + "She saw this in WEB1-CODE1: without escaping, a note's <b> became real; with html.escape it stayed text. Now the discipline: find EVERY place user data is written into a page and confirm it is escaped - better, switch to Flask's templates (render_template with Jinja), which escape automatically, so no one has to remember. Then add a Content-Security-Policy header (the school uses one - show next.config.js's CSP as the real example) that tells the browser to run no inline scripts, so even a missed escape is caught. She does not craft any attack string; she hardens her own output path and explains the two layers. Check: where escaping goes (on output), and what the CSP adds."),
    lesson("WEB2-L3", "Talking to the database safely: placeholders, and never building queries from text",
      ["Explain the weakness in one line (if a query is glued together from user text, the text could change what the query does) and point to her INSERT/SELECT that already uses ? placeholders", "Explain how a placeholder works underneath (the database gets the query shape and the values separately, so a value can never become part of the command)", "Confirm every query in her app uses placeholders and add one new query the safe way"],
      FROM_ZERO + LINUX + LAB + "WEB1-L3 told her to use ? placeholders; now she learns why they are non-negotiable. Mechanism (the defence, not an exploit): the database is handed the query's SHAPE once and the values separately, so a value is only ever treated as a value - the same idea as escaping, one layer down. She reviews each query in her app and confirms the separation; she adds a 'search notes by who' feature using a placeholder, and says out loud why she would never build that query by joining strings. No attack strings are written. Check: what a placeholder keeps separate, and why that closes the whole class."),
    lesson("WEB2-L4", "Who may do what: sessions, the owner check, and the URL that should not obey",
      ["Explain the weakness in one line (an app that shows or changes a thing just because the URL names it, without checking who is asking) and add the missing owner check", "Require login on the routes that change data; explain the difference between knowing who you are (authentication) and what you may do (authorisation)", "Add a per-form token idea and explain, as mechanism, what it defends (a request her browser makes without her meaning to)"],
      FROM_ZERO + LINUX + LAB + "Two words she will use for years: authentication (who you are - the login/cookie from WEB1-L4) and authorisation (what you may do). In her noticeboard, deleting a note should check both that she is logged in AND that it is a route only the family may call - she adds the @login_required check and a check that the method is POST, not a plain link. Then the cross-site request idea as mechanism only: a form on another page could aim at her app using her logged-in browser; the defence is a secret token in each of her own forms that an outside page cannot know - Flask-WTF or a session token. She implements the defences on her own app; nothing is aimed at anyone. Check: authentication vs authorisation, in her words."),
    lesson("WEB2-L5", "Secrets, errors and HTTPS: the last three the professionals always check",
      ["Move the app's secrets (the session key, the family password hash) out of the code into an environment file that git ignores; explain why (CS5-L2)", "Make errors generic to the user and detailed only in the server log; explain what an error message can leak", "Put the app behind HTTPS with a free certificate and explain what it protects (NET1-L4) and what it does not"],
      FROM_ZERO + LINUX + LAB + "Secrets: a .env read by the app, listed in .gitignore - the school does exactly this (show the pattern). Errors: the user sees 'something went wrong'; the log sees the detail - because a stack trace on the page tells an attacker the versions and paths (she saw the school's own friendly error page, app/error.js, built for this reason). HTTPS on the Pi: a free certificate (Let's Encrypt via certbot, or a self-signed one for the LAN with the trade-off explained) so the login cookie and password cross the Wi-Fi sealed - NET1-L4's envelope, now hers; and the honest limit: it secures the path, not the app's own logic. Check: where secrets live, what an error must not show, what HTTPS does and does not protect."),
    lesson("WEB2-L6", "The review: think like a defender, write it like a professional",
      ["Walk her own app once more with a written checklist (input checks, escaping, placeholders, auth, secrets, errors, HTTPS, logging) and tick each with the line that proves it", "Write the review as a short report: scope, what was checked, findings, fixes, what to check next", "Explain the honest limits: a review is a snapshot, and the only systems she reviews are her own or ones she is invited to"],
      FROM_ZERO + LINUX + LAB + "This is the deliverable and the habit. The checklist is the one real web-security teams use, shrunk to her app. She produces the report (the gift), exports it to PDF from her logbook. The ethics lesson stated plainly and in her words: she tests only her own machines and apps, or ones whose owner has asked her in writing; finding a weakness in someone else's system means stop, write it down, tell Abid, report it properly (DEF1-H1). Boundary Question: 'A site you use has an obvious flaw. What are the three things you do, and the one thing you never do?' Check: the checklist from memory."),
    lesson("WEB2-H1", "Horizon: web security in the world - bug bounties, standards and the defenders",
      ["Explain the OWASP Top Ten as 'the ten mistakes worth checking first', named not detailed", "Explain a bug bounty and responsible disclosure as the honest economy of finding flaws", "Ask who benefits, how would you break it, how would you fix it - for a login page, staying on the defence"],
      HORIZON + "OWASP (define: a non-profit that publishes the list of the most common web weaknesses, so every team checks the same things) - she has now met most of the list by building and hardening, not by attacking. Bug bounties: companies pay researchers who report flaws privately; the researchers who sell to criminals instead are the other side of the same skill - the fork in the road she chose at the start (builder-defender). Mechanism of the honest path: report, wait, get credited. Boundary Question for a login page, defence only: 'Name three ways a login page is attacked and the one defence that covers each' (guessing -> rate limit + lockout; stolen reused password -> 2FA; stolen cookie -> HttpOnly/HTTPS)."),
    check("WEB2-C", "Web 2 checkpoint", ["input-is-data", "escaping-output", "sql-placeholders", "auth-vs-authz", "secrets-errors-https"], count=10),
    teach("WEB2-T", "Explain to Abid, as if he ran a small website, the five things that keep a web app safe and why each one works, using one example from your own noticeboard for each. Say it out loud first, then write it. 5-7 sentences."),
  ],
  read={"url": "https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/", "title": "Read: the OWASP Top Ten - the list every web team checks against"})

